A greyscale PNG carries no PLTE, so SDL_image expands it to an indexed
surface over a ramp it synthesises itself -- and builds as
`(i * 255) / ncolors`, where the last entry has to land on 255: the
indices are the file's own grey sample values, so the divisor is the
last index, not the count. At eight bits the count-division maps grey
146 to 145 and tops the ramp out at 254, so every greyscale PNG decodes
one level dark. Measured here on a greyscale heightmap whose displaced
terrain sat one quantisation step low across most of the mesh.

`ncolors` is at least 2 on every path that reaches the ramp (the grey
formats are INDEX1/2/4/8), so the last-index division is safe, and the
1/2/4-bit ramps it produces (0,255; 0,85,170,255; i*17) are the exact
sample expansions.

This patch self-retires: once SDL_image builds the ramp over the last
index upstream, it fails to apply and the overlay is deleted.

diff --git a/src/IMG_libpng.c b/src/IMG_libpng.c
index eb3eb12..d7ed74f 100644
--- a/src/IMG_libpng.c
+++ b/src/IMG_libpng.c
@@ -479,10 +479,15 @@ static bool LIBPNG_LoadPNG_IO_Internal(SDL_IOStream *src, struct png_load_vars *
             return false;
         }
 
+        // The indices are the file's own grey sample values, so the ramp
+        // must map the last one to full white: divide by the last index,
+        // not the count. Dividing by ncolors tops an 8-bit ramp out at
+        // 254 and lands grey 146 on 145 -- every level above zero comes
+        // back one shade dark.
         for (int i = 0; i < palette->ncolors; i++) {
-            palette->colors[i].r = (i * 255) / palette->ncolors;
-            palette->colors[i].g = (i * 255) / palette->ncolors;
-            palette->colors[i].b = (i * 255) / palette->ncolors;
+            palette->colors[i].r = (i * 255) / (palette->ncolors - 1);
+            palette->colors[i].g = (i * 255) / (palette->ncolors - 1);
+            palette->colors[i].b = (i * 255) / (palette->ncolors - 1);
             palette->colors[i].a = 255;
         }
     }
